Proposed API operations#
POST /registrations — register an authenticated student for a section.
GET /registrations — list the authenticated student's registrations.
DELETE /registrations/<enrollment_id> — withdraw an authorized enrollment.
Authorization must ensure students can modify only their own enrollments. Enrollment should be processed in a database transaction to avoid oversubscribing sections when multiple students register simultaneously.

Section 3: Structure of the Related Database Table#
Table name: enrollments
Database location: Shared PostgreSQL database service

Column	PostgreSQL type	Constraint	Purpose
enrollment_id	BIGINT	Primary key, generated identity	Unique registration identifier
student_id	BIGINT	NOT NULL, foreign key	Student who registers
section_id	BIGINT	NOT NULL, foreign key	Course section selected
status	VARCHAR(20)	NOT NULL, checked	enrolled or withdrawn
enrolled_at	TIMESTAMPTZ	NOT NULL, default current time	Registration timestamp
CREATE TABLE enrollments (
    enrollment_id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    student_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES students(student_id),
    section_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES course_sections(section_id),
    status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'enrolled'
        CHECK (status IN ('enrolled', 'withdrawn')),
    enrolled_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    UNIQUE (student_id, section_id)
);
Relationships: Each enrollment belongs to one student and one course section. The referenced students and course_sections tables must exist before this table is created. The unique constraint prevents duplicate enrollment rows for the same student and section; a withdrawn enrollment may be reactivated according to application policy.

Database operations: Create a registration, read current registrations, and update a registration to withdrawn. Capacity checks use the course_sections table and must be transaction-safe.

